    "xpBounty": 80,
    "paradigm": "Lock-Free Hardware CAS & Work-Stealing Task Pools",
    "coreConcept": "Explicit ReentrantLock with timed tryLock, ExecutorService thread pools, ForkJoinPool work-stealing, and lock-free atomic CAS variables",
    "timeComplexity": "O(1) lock-free CAS, O(1) task submission",
    "spaceComplexity": "Pool worker queue footprint",
    "description": "High-level modern concurrency: ReentrantLock timed polling, thread pool executors, work-stealing ForkJoinPool, and lock-free AtomicInteger CAS.",
    "theory": "# Module 19: Concurrency III \u2014 ReentrantLock, Executor Pools, ForkJoin & Atomic CAS\n\n> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials \u2014 *Essential Java Classes*  \n> **Source Lessons:** `concurrency/highlevel.html`, `newlocks.html`, `executors.html`, `pools.html`, `forkjoin.html`, `collections.html`, `atomicvars.html`, `threadlocalrandom.html`  \n> **Scope:** Explicit Locks (`ReentrantLock`), Timed `tryLock()`, `ExecutorService` Thread Pools, Graceful Shutdown, `ForkJoinPool` Work-Stealing, Atomic CAS Variables (`AtomicInteger`), `ConcurrentHashMap`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Overview: The `java.util.concurrent` Ecosystem\n\nModern Java concurrency avoids low-level `Thread` creation and `synchronized` blocks in favor of high-performance utilities:\n1. **Explicit Locks (`ReentrantLock`):** Provide timed lock polling (`tryLock`), interruptible locks, and fairness policies.\n2. **Executors & Thread Pools:** Decouple task submission from thread management.\n3. **Lock-Free Atomic Variables:** Hardware-level Compare-And-Swap (CAS) instructions eliminating lock contention.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    JUC[\"java.util.concurrent Framework\"]\n    JUC --> Locks[\"ReentrantLock & ReadWriteLock\"]\n    JUC --> Exec[\"ExecutorService & ForkJoinPool\"]\n    JUC --> CAS[\"AtomicInteger / AtomicReference (Lock-Free)\"]\n    JUC --> Coll[\"ConcurrentHashMap (Lock-Striped)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exhaustive Member Function & JUC Reference\n\n| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Timed TryLock** | `lock.tryLock(t, u)` | `if (lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS))` | Timed acquisition | Attempts lock acquisition within deadline, avoiding deadlocks. |\n| **Executor Submit** | `exec.submit(callable)`| `Future<V> f = pool.submit(task);` | O(1) queue push | Submits task and returns a `Future` handle. |\n| **Atomic CAS** | `atomic.incrementAndGet()`| `int next = seq.incrementAndGet();` | O(1) lock-free | Hardware atomic increment using Compare-And-Swap. |\n| **Concurrent Map** | `map.putIfAbsent(k, v)`| `map.computeIfAbsent(k, f);` | O(1) bucket lock | Thread-safe associative map without global locking. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation\n\n```java\n// Comprehensive demonstration of ExecutorService, ReentrantLock, and Atomic CAS\npackage devtype.engine.concurrency;\n\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\nimport java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;\n\npublic final class AdvancedConcurrencyLab {\n\n    public static class MetricsCollector {\n        private final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);\n        public long recordRequest() { return counter.incrementAndGet(); } // Hardware CAS\n        public long getCount() { return counter.get(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void runExecutorTasks() {\n        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);\n        try {\n            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n                final int id = i;\n                pool.submit(() -> System.out.println(\"Running task: \" + id));\n            }\n        } finally {\n            pool.shutdown();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices\n\n1. **Calling `Future.get()` in Submit Loops:**\n   - *Trap:* Invoking `future.get()` immediately after submitting a task blocks the caller, serializing what was intended to be concurrent execution.\n   - *Remedy:* Submit all tasks first, collect the list of `Future` handles, then await completion.",
    "code": "package devtype.engine.concurrency;\n\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\n\npublic final class AdvancedConcurrencyLab {\n\n    public static class MetricsCollector {\n        private final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);\n        public long recordRequest() { return counter.incrementAndGet(); }\n        public long getCount() { return counter.get(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void runExecutorTasks() {\n        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);\n        try {\n            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n                final int id = i;\n                pool.submit(() -> System.out.println(\"Running task: \" + id));\n            }\n        } finally {\n            pool.shutdown();\n        }\n    }\n}"
