    "coreConcept": "NFA backtracking mechanics, character class unions/intersections, greedy vs reluctant vs possessive quantifiers, and ReDoS prevention",
    "timeComplexity": "O(N) search with possessive quantifiers, O(2^N) catastrophic greedy backtracking",
    "spaceComplexity": "NFA state machine graph allocation",
    "description": "Deep architectural analysis of the Java Regex NFA engine: character classes, greedy vs reluctant vs possessive quantifiers, and ReDoS prevention.",
    "theory": "# Module 22: Regular Expressions I \u2014 NFA Engine, Character Classes & Quantifiers (ReDoS)\n\n> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials \u2014 *Essential Java Classes*  \n> **Source Lessons:** `regex/index.html`, `intro.html`, `char_classes.html`, `pre_char_classes.html`, `quant.html`, `pse.html`  \n> **Scope:** NFA Backtracking Engine, Character Classes (Unions, Intersections, Subtractions), Predefined Classes, Greedy vs Reluctant vs Possessive Quantifiers, Catastrophic Backtracking (ReDoS).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Overview & The Three Quantifier Engines\n\nJava regex (`java.util.regex`) uses a **Nondeterministic Finite Automaton (NFA)** engine. Quantifiers govern how many times an element may repeat:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Q[\"Quantifier Operational Modes\"]\n    Q --> Greedy[\"1. GREEDY (X*, X+, X{n,})<br/>Reads entire input first.<br/>Backtracks one char at a time.\"]\n    Q --> Reluctant[\"2. RELUCTANT / LAZY (X*?, X+?, X{n,}?)<br/>Reads one char at a time.<br/>Expands forward upon failure.\"]\n    Q --> Possessive[\"3. POSSESSIVE (X*+, X++, X{n,}+)<br/>Reads entire input like greedy.<br/>NEVER BACKTRACKS! (No ReDoS)\"]\n```\n\n### Catastrophic Backtracking & ReDoS Prevention\nWhen nested greedy quantifiers (e.g. `(a+)+$`) encounter a non-matching string like `\"aaaaaaaaaaaaab\"`, the NFA explores $2^N$ combinatorial paths, pegging CPU at 100% indefinitely (**Regular Expression Denial of Service**).\n- *Solution:* Use **Possessive Quantifiers (`X++`, `X*+`)** to completely disable backtracking.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exhaustive Member Function & Character Class Reference\n\n| Category | Syntax / Pattern (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Character Class** | `[a-z]` | `Pattern.compile(\"[0-9a-fA-F]\");` | O(1) table check | Matches any character within range. |\n| **Intersection** | `[a-z&&[def]]` | `Pattern.compile(\"[a-z&&[^aeiou]]\");`| O(1) bitwise | Matches characters belonging to both sets (e.g. consonants). |\n| **Predefined Class**| `\\d`, `\\w`, `\\s` | `Pattern.compile(\"\\\\w+@\\\\w+\\\\.\\\\w+\");` | O(1) lookup | Shorthand for digits, word characters, and whitespace. |\n| **Possessive Quant**| `X++` | `Pattern.compile(\"\\\\d++\");` | O(N) zero backtrack | Matches greedily without backtracking; eliminates ReDoS vulnerabilities. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation\n\n```java\n// Comprehensive demonstration of possessive quantifiers and character class filtering\npackage devtype.engine.regex;\n\nimport java.util.regex.*;\n\npublic final class RegexBasicsLab {\n\n    // Possessive quantifier prevents catastrophic backtracking\n    private static final Pattern HEX_PATTERN = Pattern.compile(\"^[0-9a-fA-F]++$\");\n\n    public static boolean isValidHex(String text) {\n        if (text == null) return false;\n        return HEX_PATTERN.matcher(text).matches();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices\n\n1. **Recompiling `Pattern.compile()` in Loops:**\n   - *Trap:* Compiling regex strings repeatedly inside loops incurs massive CPU overhead reconstructing the NFA graph.\n   - *Remedy:* Compile once as `private static final Pattern`.",
    "code": "package devtype.engine.regex;\n\nimport java.util.regex.*;\n\npublic final class RegexBasicsLab {\n\n    // Possessive quantifier prevents catastrophic backtracking\n    private static final Pattern HEX_PATTERN = Pattern.compile(\"^[0-9a-fA-F]++$\");\n\n    public static boolean isValidHex(String text) {\n        if (text == null) return false;\n        return HEX_PATTERN.matcher(text).matches();\n    }\n}"
